<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashanti Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Asante Empire, Asante, Ashanti, Asante Kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ashanti Empire was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> around the end of the seventeenth century with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. Its power rested on gold, trade, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and a strong army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> symbolised its unity. After a series of wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> it was annexed in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire.jpg" alt="Ashanti Empire" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asante_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early nineteenth century <b>Asante</b>, the most powerful state of the Akan interior, pressed southward to protect its trade: it invaded the coast in 1807, fought the Ga-Fante War of 1811 and the Ashanti–Akim–Akwapim War of 1814–16, and in 1817 the African Company of Merchants recognised its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…linked the caliphate to Tripoli and other North African termini across the desert, westward to the kola-producing forests of <b>Asante</b>, southward to the Yoruba markets, and eastward to Bornu and the Lake Chad basin, with cloth, leather goods, kola nuts and slaves among the principal commodities of exchange.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Asante</b>, an Akan people, ranked their people by lineage, by office and by freedom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Akyem Abuakwa allied with the British against <b>Ashanti</b> and came under British rule with the Gold Coast Colony in 1874.</blockquote><a href="/state/akyem-abuakwa">Akyem Abuakwa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1900 the governor's demand for the Golden Stool, the sacred symbol of the <b>Asante</b> nation, provoked a rising led by Yaa Asantewaa, queen mother of Ejisu.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people and royal stools were absorbed into the <b>Ashanti</b> system, and the Techiman traditional state claims succession from Bono.</blockquote><a href="/state/bono-state">Bono State</a></li></ul></section>
